<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bangor Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 558</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bangor Abbey was a monastery on the southern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belfast">Belfast</a> Lough in County Down, Northern Ireland, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-comgall">Saint Comgall</a> around 558. It became one of the great schools of early Irish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, sending out missionaries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbanus">Columbanus</a> and producing the Antiphonary of Bangor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids devastated it in the 9th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bangor-abbey.jpg" alt="Bangor Abbey" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Notafly · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BangorAbbeyAD688.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
